  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Stable Physical Systems",
    "lesson_core_move": "Interpret a stable physical continuous-time system as one whose response remains finite because its dynamics prevent bounded inputs from accumulating without bound.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Stable Physical Systems",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Stable Physical Systems",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A stable physical continuous-time system is one whose response stays finite over time when the input is bounded. The cue is not the size of the input alone; it is whether the system's own dynamics let the effect settle instead of pile up.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|x(t)| \\le M_x \\text{ for all } t \\implies |y(t)| \\le M_y \\text{ for all } t.",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In physical terms, stability comes from internal dynamics that dissipate or leak energy, so bounded input does not keep accumulating into a larger and larger response. Decay, leakage, and dissipation are the mechanisms that keep the long-run output under control.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=y_{\\mathrm{steady}}(t)+y_{\\mathrm{transient}}(t), \\qquad y_{\\mathrm{transient}}(t)\\to 0 \\text{ as } t\\to\\infty",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When the transient decays, the response remains inside a finite envelope instead of accumulating without bound. That is the basic stability idea the next examples will use in more detailed cases.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Stable Physical Systems",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Recognizing Finite Responses in Physical Systems",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Classify the response $y(t)=3 + 2e^{-4t}+0.1\\cos(2t)$ as stable or not under bounded physical input assumptions.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue here is a response law made from a constant offset, a decaying exponential, and a bounded oscillation. To classify the system, check whether any part can grow without bound as time advances.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=3+2e^{-4t}+0.1\\cos(2t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Each term stays finite: $3$ is a constant, $2e^{-4t}$ decays because the exponent is negative, and $0.1\\cos(2t)$ always remains between $-0.1$ and $0.1$. None of these pieces can accumulate into unbounded growth.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|y(t)| \\le |3| + |2e^{-4t}| + |0.1\\cos(2t)| \\le 3 + 2 + 0.1 = 5.1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the response remains inside a finite envelope for all time, with the transient term decaying and the oscillation staying bounded. That is the stable physical behavior: bounded input does not turn into unbounded accumulation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Stable Physical Systems",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Bounded Responses to Sustained Inputs",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $\\dot y+5y=x(t)$, $y(0)=0$, and $|x(t)|\\le 1$, classify whether $y(t)$ remains finite for all $t$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a first-order system with a damping term $+5y$ and a bounded input $|x(t)|\\le 1$. To decide stability, write the response so the decaying natural part is separated from the forced part.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=e^{-5t}y(0)+\\int_0^t e^{-5(t-\\tau)}x(\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here $y(0)=0$, so the homogeneous term drops out and the response is the input filtered by a decaying kernel. Because $e^{-5(t-\\tau)}$ shrinks older input instead of letting it accumulate, a bounded input cannot build into an unbounded output.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|y(t)| \\le \\int_0^t e^{-5(t-\\tau)}|x(\\tau)|\\,d\\tau \\le \\int_0^t e^{-5(t-\\tau)}\\,d\\tau = \\frac{1-e^{-5t}}5 \\le \\frac15",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $y(t)$ remains finite for all $t$ and stays inside a fixed envelope. The system is stable because its dynamics keep each bounded contribution from piling up without bound.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Stable Physical Systems",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Settling Behavior After a Bounded Input Change",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For a bounded input shift producing $y(t)=2+Ce^{-3t}$, determine finite settling behavior after the change and justify with dynamics.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a bounded input change whose response splits into a steady term and a transient term. To decide settling behavior, identify the long-time value and check whether the transient decays.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=2+Ce^{-3t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The constant term $2$ is the finite settled level, and the transient $Ce^{-3t}$ decays because the exponent is negative. Since there is no polynomial growth term, nothing in the response can build up without bound after the input change.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\lim_{t\\to\\infty} y(t)=2, \\qquad y(t)-2=Ce^{-3t}\\to 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the response settles to a finite value after the bounded input change, and the settling comes from the decaying dynamics rather than from the initial condition alone. This is stable physical behavior: a bounded change produces a bounded transient that fades into a finite steady state.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Stable Physical Systems",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Identifying Dynamics That Prevent Accumulation",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compare $y_1(t)=\\int_0^t x(\\tau)\\,d\\tau$ and $y_2(t)=\\int_0^t e^{-2(t-\\tau)}x(\\tau)\\,d\\tau$ for bounded $x(t)$ and decide which prevents accumulation.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is two responses built from past input, but only one has a decaying memory of the past. To test accumulation, check whether older input keeps the same weight or gets suppressed with time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_1(t)=\\int_0^t x(\\tau)\\,d\\tau, \\qquad y_2(t)=\\int_0^t e^{-2(t-\\tau)}x(\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In $y_1$, every past input contributes equally, so bounded input can keep stacking up. In $y_2$, the factor $e^{-2(t-\\tau)}$ makes older input count less, so the response does not keep full memory of the entire history.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=1 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; y_1(t)=\\int_0^t 1\\,d\\tau=t, \\qquad y_2(t)=\\int_0^t e^{-2(t-\\tau)}\\,d\\tau=\\frac{1-e^{-2t}}{2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This shows the decision directly: $y_1$ is accumulation-prone because a bounded sustained input can grow without bound, while $y_2$ stays finite because the decaying kernel prevents old input from piling up. The dynamics in $y_2$ are the anti-accumulation mechanism.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
