        "image_slots": [],
        "question_count": 2,
        "questions": [
          {
            "question_index": 1,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Compare the two bounded-input response forms. With only the fact that $x(t)$ is bounded, which form better prevents accumulation of past input for all time?",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              },
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "$y_1(t)=\\int_0^t x(\\tau)\\,d\\tau$ and $y_2(t)=\\int_0^t e^{-2(t-\\tau)}x(\\tau)\\,d\\tau$",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_2$ prevents accumulation because the factor $e^{-2(t-\\tau)}$ suppresses older input and gives a finite response for bounded input. ",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": true
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "B",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_1$ is safer because it is an integral and all integrals stay finite if inputs are bounded.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "C",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "Both $y_1$ and $y_2$ are equivalent for bounded inputs because both depend on an integral from $0$ to $t$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "D",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "Only the sign of $x(t)$ matters; since $x(t)$ might be positive for long times, both rules fail the anti-accumulation test.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "E",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "Neither form can be judged without exact initial conditions of $y(t)$ before $t=0$.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              }
            ],
            "correct_choice_labels": [
              "A"
            ],
            "correct_answer": "A",
            "answer_explanation_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "For a bounded input, the accumulation-prone term is the one with equal weighting over all past input. If $x(t)=1$, then $y_1(t)=t$ grows without bound while $y_2(t)=\\int_0^t e^{-2(t-\\tau)}d\\tau=(1-e^{-2t})/2$ is bounded. So $y_2$ is the anti-accumulation form.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "question_index": 2,
            "question_type": "multiple-choice",
            "answer_cardinality": "single",
            "prompt_blocks": [
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "With bounded input $x(t)=\\sin t$, compare these outputs: $y_1(t)=\\int_0^t e^{-0.5(t-\\tau)}x(\\tau)\\,d\\tau$ and $y_2(t)=\\int_0^t e^{+0.5(t-\\tau)}x(\\tau)\\,d\\tau$. Which choice is correct?",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              },
              {
                "block_type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Choose the best statement about bounded-input accumulation.",
                "latex": "",
                "image_slot_id": ""
              }
            ],
            "choices": [
              {
                "choice_label": "A",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_1$ is accumulation-preventing while $y_2$ can be unbounded because the kernel grows with age of input.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": true
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "B",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_2$ is accumulation-preventing and $y_1$ is unstable because bounded output is impossible for sinusoidal inputs.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "C",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "Both are equivalent: neither can be distinguished using boundedness because both are convolutions.",
                    "latex": "",
                    "image_slot_id": ""
                  }
                ],
                "is_correct": false
              },
              {
                "choice_label": "D",
                "choice_blocks": [
                  {
                    "block_type": "paragraph",
                    "text": "$y_1$ is unstable because the exponential factor is still an integral, so accumulation always happens.",
